-- title:
--   Advance the native graph dynamic program through its remaining layers
-- statement:
--   Let $k\ge1$. Suppose the core native invariant holds for layer one, the layer index is zero, the layer counter contains $k-1$ markers and all vertex, state and predecessor loop counters are empty. The layer loop consumes this fuel and reaches layer index $k-1$, with its previous-layer database equal to the framed arrays of
--   $$
--   \operatorname{denseLayer}(k,v,a),\qquad v<n,\ a<k.
--   $$
--   It preserves the graph, cached signs, templates and all saved tails. With $W=100(1+n+k)^2$ and $m$ stored arcs, its budget is
--   $$
--   \bigl(C_{\rm layer}(n,k,m)+13000(W+1)^2+31\bigr)(k-1)+30.
--   $$
--   Here $C_{\rm layer}$ is the verified cost of computing and installing one later layer. The theorem includes $k=1$, when no later layer is needed.
-- source:
--   T. Koana, Approximate Counting of k-Paths in O*(2^k) Time, STOC 2027 submission #953, October 2026, §3.2 (printed pp. 8–9 / PDF pp. 9–10) and §4.1 (printed p. 9 / PDF p. 10), https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-2/953.pdf. Native implementation: Solutions/LayerLoop.lean, line 21; source snapshot SHA-256 5c18fcbb01fbd96896bf4c05c78918dd66f3e46608ed839be9d6eae6d5067499.

